BRAIN UNIT — POWER + DISPLAY
Full picture of every wire: see "Wiring diagram.png" / "Wiring diagram.svg" in this repo.
1. Power path (battery → ESP32-S3)
Battery → TP4056 charger → switch → MT3608 boost (5.00V) → ESP32-S3 "5V" pin

Why every part is needed:

Charger board – charges the cell and protects it from over-discharge/short. Required.
Switch – turns the whole unit off, and lets you charge with the unit off. Required.
Boost converter – a LiPo is 3.0–4.2V. The board's 5V pin needs a real 5V. Required.
Capacitor – smooths the boost output so the ESP32 doesn't reset during BLE/display bursts. Strongly recommended.

Note on 700mAh: ESP32-S3 + display will pull roughly 0.5–0.7A from the battery at short peaks. That's near 1C for a 700mAh cell. It will work, but check the battery for warmth in the first test. Runtime is NOT measured yet — rough estimate 1.5–3 hours with BLE + screen on. Measure it in Step 3 before trusting any number.

Charge-current warning (CHECK BEFORE FIRST CHARGE):
Most TP4056 boards come set to about 0.9–1A charging (programming resistor, usually labeled R3, marked "122" = 1.2kΩ). That is more than 1C for a 700mAh cell, which is above what most small LiPos are rated for.
- Check your battery's max charge current (label or listing). If it is 1C or lower (≤700mA), change R3 to 2kΩ (marked "202") → about 580mA. 3kΩ ("302") → about 400mA, even gentler.
- If you can't change the resistor yet: charge only while you're watching, on a non-flammable surface, and stop if the battery gets warm or puffs.
TP4056 RPROG table: 1.2kΩ ≈ 0.9–1A, 2kΩ ≈ 580mA, 5kΩ ≈ 250mA.

2. Power wiring — exact connections

Before soldering anything: meter on the battery leads. Whichever lead reads positive is +. Ignore wire color.

From	To
Battery +	Charger B+
Battery –	Charger B–
Charger OUT+	Switch middle pin
Switch right pin	Boost VIN+
Charger OUT–	Boost VIN–
Switch left pin	nothing — heat-shrink it

STOP HERE. Switch on. Meter on VOUT+ / VOUT–. Turn the blue screw until it reads 5.00V. (Many turns — it's slow. If it doesn't move, keep turning; the trimmer often needs 10+ turns before anything changes.) Switch off, then on again, confirm it's still 5.00V.

Then continue:

From	To
Boost VOUT+	ESP32-S3 pin marked 5V
Boost VOUT–	ESP32-S3 pin marked GND
Capacitor + (long leg / no stripe)	Boost VOUT+
Capacitor – (stripe side)	Boost VOUT–

Capacitor: 220–470µF electrolytic, rated 10V or more. Solder it right at the boost output pads. No ceramic capacitor needed — the MT3608 and ESP32 boards already have small ceramics on board. (Optional later: a 0.1µF ceramic across the same pads only if you get random resets.)

Hard rule: When the switch is ON, do not plug USB into the ESP32. Two 5V sources fighting can damage the board. USB only when the switch is OFF.

3. Display wiring (LCD → ESP32-S3)
Display pin	ESP32-S3 pin
GND	GND
VCC	3V3 (see section 4)
SCK	GPIO 12
SDA	GPIO 11
RST	GPIO 10
DC	GPIO 9
CS	GPIO 8

Wires under 10cm. Never connect or disconnect the display while the board is powered.

Verified for this exact module (GMT024-10 V2.1, 2.4", 240x320):
- Controller: ST7789. SPI, 3.3V logic → wires go straight to ESP32 GPIOs, no level shifter.
- 7 pins total. There is NO backlight (BLK/LED) pin — the backlight is tied to VCC inside the module, so it turns on whenever VCC is powered. Nothing else to wire.
- There is NO MISO pin → TFT_MISO -1 is correct.
- "SDA" is SPI data (MOSI), not I2C.

4. LCD VCC: 3.3V or 5V?

Use 3.3V.

The seller spec for GMT024-10 V2.1 says VCC 3.3V–5V, logic 3.3V. So 3.3V is inside the rated range, and it's the choice that cannot hurt the display even if you got a different variant.

Optional 30-second check: flip the display over. A tiny 3-pin chip near VCC (marked 662K, 4A2, or similar) is the regulator that makes 5V allowed. Either way, still use 3.3V.

VCC → 3V3. Never 5V on any signal pin (SCK, SDA, RST, DC, CS). The board's 3.3V regulator has plenty of margin for the ~60–80mA the screen draws.

5. Are GPIO 8–12 safe on this board?

Yes. Checked against the ESP32-S3 datasheet:

Boot/strapping pins are 0, 3, 45, 46 — not used here.
USB is 19, 20 — not used.
Flash is 26–32 — not used, not broken out.
PSRAM (the R8 part) is 33–37 — not used. Note: 35, 36, 37 ARE on the header on many boards, but on N16R8 they are wired to the PSRAM. Never use them.
UART0 is 43, 44 — not used.
Onboard RGB LED is 48 (sometimes 38) — not used.
GPIO 10, 11, 12 are actually the chip's native SPI pins — best possible choice for a display.

Nothing to worry about. Do not change these to anything in the ranges above.

6. Test before permanent wiring

A. Power chain, no ESP32 attached

Boost reads 5.00V unloaded ✔
Switch OFF → VIN+ reads 0V ✔
Charger: plug USB-C into the charger board with the switch OFF. Red/blue LED lights, board barely warm. ✔
Meter between the wire going to the "5V" pin and the wire going to "GND": reads +5.00V (positive, not negative). ✔

B. Display on USB (no battery yet)
Wire display per section 3. Plug ESP32 USB. Run the test sketch below.

C. Battery last
Unplug USB. Connect boost output. Switch on. Board boots, display works.

7. Step-by-step order
Step 1 — ESP32 alone, USB

Arduino IDE board settings (Tools menu):
- Board: ESP32S3 Dev Module
- Flash Size: 16MB
- PSRAM: OPI PSRAM (N16R8 = octal PSRAM. "QSPI PSRAM" is wrong for this board)
- USB CDC On Boot: Disabled (when using the UART/COM port)

Plug in the UART USB port. Upload a blank sketch with Serial.println("alive"). Confirm serial output. Done.

Step 2 — Display, still USB

Wire the 7 display wires. Install TFT_eSPI library. In its User_Setup.h, comment out everything else and set:

#define ST7789_DRIVER        // try this first
#define TFT_WIDTH  240
#define TFT_HEIGHT 320
#define USE_HSPI_PORT        // REQUIRED on ESP32-S3 with ESP32 board package 2.0.15 or newer (incl. 3.x)
#define TFT_MOSI 11
#define TFT_SCLK 12
#define TFT_CS    8
#define TFT_DC    9
#define TFT_RST  10
#define TFT_MISO -1
#define LOAD_GLCD
#define SPI_FREQUENCY 27000000

Test sketch:

#include <TFT_eSPI.h>
TFT_eSPI tft;
void setup() {
  tft.init(); tft.setRotation(0);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_RED);   delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_GREEN); delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_BLUE);  delay(500);
  tft.fillScreen(TFT_BLACK);
  tft.setTextColor(TFT_WHITE); tft.setTextSize(3);
  tft.drawString("OK", 80, 140);
}
void loop() {}

Why USE_HSPI_PORT is required: in ESP32 board package 3.x, FSPI = 0 on the S3. TFT_eSPI's default uses FSPI as its register address, which points to address 0 → the board crashes / boot-loops at tft.init(). USE_HSPI_PORT makes it use the S3's second SPI bus (SPI3), which works with any pins including 8–12. (Verified: compiles on ESP32 core 3.3.12 + TFT_eSPI 2.5.43; also compiles together with the NimBLE keyboard code.)
Back up your User_Setup.h — updating TFT_eSPI overwrites it.

Red, green, blue, "OK" → driver correct. Done.
Board reboots in a loop / "Guru Meditation" at tft.init() → USE_HSPI_PORT is missing or User_Setup.h wasn't saved.
Colors wrong/inverted → add #define TFT_INVERSION_ON (or #define TFT_INVERSION_OFF), or #define TFT_RGB_ORDER TFT_BGR.
Garbage/blank → this module is verified ST7789, so re-check the 7 wires first. Then try ST7789_2_DRIVER. Wrong driver can't damage anything, only shows junk.
Still blank → backlight on but no image = wiring order; check SCK/SDA aren't swapped.
Step 3 — Battery power
Unplug USB.
Connect boost VOUT+ → 5V, VOUT– → GND (with capacitor already on).
Switch ON. Display shows the test.
Meter across the 5V/GND pins while it runs: should stay 4.8–5.1V.
Run 15 minutes. Touch the boost inductor, the battery, the ESP32 regulator. Warm is fine. Too hot to hold = stop.
Switch OFF, charge via charger USB-C, confirm the charge LED changes when full.

Only after this passes do you put anything in the tin.

Don't
Don't plug ESP32 USB in while the switch is on.
Don't put 5V on VCC — 3V3 only.
Don't touch the blue screw once the ESP32 is connected.
Don't connect/disconnect the display while powered.
Don't let any board underside touch the tin.
